## Problem1('')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros(
  "PGstandard.pl",
  "MathObjects.pl",
  "PGcourse.pl"
);

TEXT(beginproblem());
$showPartialCorrectAnswers = 1;

Context('Matrix')->variables->are(n => ['Real', limits=>[-5,5], resolution=>1]);

$a = non_zero_random(-2,2,1);
$b = 2/$a;
$s = random(-1,1,2);
$c = random(2,9,1);
$d = random(2,9,1);
if ($c == $d) {$d = $d+1;}

$m11 = 2*$c-$d;
$m12 = $s*$a*$d - $s*$a*$c;
$m21 = $s*$b*$c - $s*$b*$d;
$m22 = 2*$d - $c;

$ans11 = Compute("2*($c**n) - $d**n");
$ans12 = Compute("$s*$a*($d**n) - $s*$a*($c**n)");
$ans21 = Compute("$s*$b*($c**n) - $s*$b*($d**n)");
$ans22 = Compute("2*($d**n) - $c**n");

$M = Matrix([[$m11, $m12], [$m21, $m22]]);

$ans = Matrix([[$ans11,$ans12],[$ans21,$ans22]]);

Context()->texStrings;
BEGIN_TEXT
Consider the matrix \( M = $M. \)
Diagonalize \(M\) to find a formula for the entries of \(M^n\), where \(n\) is a positive integer.
$BR
$BR
$BCENTER
\(M^n=\) \{ $ans->ans_array(30) \}
$ECENTER
END_TEXT
Context()->normalStrings;

ANS( $ans->cmp );

;
ENDDOCUMENT();

statement.p:
First, find a matrix that satisfies
M 
\left[ 
\begin{array}{c}
a_{n-2}  \\
a_{n-1} 
\end{array} 
\right] 
=
\left[ 
\begin{array}{c}
a_{n-1}  \\
a_n 
\end{array} 
\right].

Note that
\left[ 
\begin{array}{c}
a_n  \\
a_{n+1} 
\end{array} 
\right] = M ^ { n } 
\left[ 
\begin{array}{c}
a_0  \\
a_1 
\end{array} 
\right].
We can use the diagonalization to find an explicit formula for a_n.

